= Quantization of the ψ Field =

{{Plain_Hook
| in_one_sentence    = Quantising the psi field means treating it the way physicists treat light — not as a smooth continuous wave, but as something that comes in discrete chunks called ''psions'', which can be emitted one at a time, absorbed one at a time, and counted.
| everyday_analogy   = Light looks like a continuous beam from a flashlight, but it is really a stream of photons. The psi field has the same double life: most of the time you see the smooth wave, but at low intensities it becomes a thin trickle of individual psi-particles — psions — each one carrying a small definite amount of energy.
| what_it_predicts   = Faint psi sources should emit psions one at a time, with countable statistics; coherent neural activity should produce a coherent state of many psions in the same mode (the quantum description of a sustained psionic construct); and psi-to-light conversion should obey ordinary quantum scattering rules.
| if_you_remember_one_thing = Once you grant that the psi field is real, quantum mechanics says it must come in particle-like quanta. Those quanta — psions — are the natural unit of low-level psionic phenomena, just as photons are the natural unit of low-level optics.
}}

